description:
Where: crates/wiac-core/src/pipeline/setup_resolver.rs:285-293.

Problem: When OpKind::Chamfer is resolved, the setup overrides force:
    setup.mill.depth = z;
    setup.mill.start_depth = 0.0;
    setup.mill.step = z;            // step == depth => single-pass
    setup.mill.finish_step = None;
    setup.mill.depth_list = Vec::new();

i.e. ANY chamfer of any depth runs as one pass from z=0 to z=depth. For a 2mm chamfer width on 90 V-bit that is -2mm (fine). For a 5mm chamfer width on 60 V-bit that is -8.66mm (catastrophic single-pass plunge into wood/aluminium with a V-bit).

The comment claims chamfer is "a constant-Z toolpath" so it does not need step-down. That is wrong: it is constant-Z along the contour, but the INITIAL plunge from start_depth=0 to z=-8.66mm is a vertical move into solid stock. The cutter has to get to operating depth first; that requires a stepdown schedule like any other op.

Real-world impact: Broken V-bit on the FIRST chamfer move when width is large. Or burning/melting on aluminium when the bit catches.

