# P3 Phase 1 — concrete Rust sketch (multi-span Z-dexel core)

*Illustrative, not compile-final. Slots next to the real symbols in
`crates/ivac-core/src/sim/heightmap.rs` and `sim/sweep.rs`. Designed so the
3-axis top-down path stays byte-for-byte identical to today.*

## 0. The one invariant that keeps the hot path free

> A column with a single full-height solid span `[stock_bottom_z, top]` is
> represented by the **dense `top` array alone** and never touches the sidecar.
> `top[idx]` always mirrors the highest solid surface, so every existing reader
> (mesh build, LOD min-pool, collision checks, `sample()`, STL export) keeps
> working unchanged. The sidecar exists only for columns that actually grew an
> undercut.

## 1. Span + tool interval

```rust
/// A solid interval along a column, Z up. Invariant: lo < hi.
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, PartialEq)]
pub struct Span { pub lo: f32, pub hi: f32 }

impl ToolProfile {
    /// Removed material interval at radial offset `r`, measured as height
    /// ABOVE THE TOOL TIP, or `None` if the cutter's solid of revolution
    /// doesn't reach radius `r`. `hi == f32::INFINITY` means "no modeled
    /// ceiling — remove up to the stock top" (the flute length isn't a cap
    /// for this tool). The LOWER surface reuses today's `eval()` verbatim,
    /// so simple tools are unchanged; only the UPPER surface is new.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn eval_interval(&self, r: f32) -> Option<(f32, f32)> {
        let lo = self.eval(r)?;              // <-- existing lower-surface math, untouched
        let hi = match self {
            // Endmill/ball/V/bull/drag/laser/compression/engraver:
            // a top-down solid column. No modeled ceiling in Phase 1, so
            // subtracting [lo, +inf] from the top span == today's min().
            ToolProfile::FormProfile { segments } => form_upper(segments, r),
            _ => f32::INFINITY,
        };
        Some((lo, hi))
    }
}

/// Upper cutting surface of a form / T-slot / dovetail cutter at radius `r`.
/// The `segments` list gains a paired "top" sample so a disk that sits at
/// z ∈ [disk_bottom, disk_top] for r ∈ [neck_r, disk_r] removes only that
/// band and leaves the overhang above the neck intact. (Phase 1 can carry a
/// second Vec<(f32,f32)> on FormProfile, or fold top+bottom into one list.)
fn form_upper(segments: &[(f32, f32)], r: f32) -> f32 { /* interp, mirror of eval() */ }
```

`FormProfile` is the only kind that returns a finite `hi`, so **T-slot and
dovetail become the first honestly-simulated undercuts** while every other tool
provably reduces to current behavior (`[eval(r), +inf]`).

## 2. The hybrid field (evolves `Heightmap`)

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use smallvec::SmallVec;

pub struct DexelField {
    pub origin: Point2,
    pub cell: f64,
    pub cols: u32,
    pub rows: u32,
    pub top_z: f32,           // uncut stock surface (== Heightmap::top_z)
    pub stock_bottom_z: f32,  // was implicit; now explicit for span bottoms

    /// DENSE fast path — identical semantics/layout to today's
    /// `Heightmap::data`: highest solid Z per column. Zero-copy to WASM/GL.
    top: Vec<f32>,

    /// SPARSE sidecar — present ONLY for columns with an interior void.
    /// Absent column ⇒ single span [stock_bottom_z, top[idx]].
    /// Present column ⇒ authoritative sorted, disjoint, ascending spans;
    /// `top[idx]` still mirrors spans.last().hi for the dense readers.
    undercut: HashMap<u32, SmallVec<[Span; 2]>>,

    dirty: Option<(u32, u32, u32, u32)>,
}

impl DexelField {
    #[inline]
    fn spans_for(&self, idx: usize) -> SmallVec<[Span; 2]> {
        match self.undercut.get(&(idx as u32)) {
            Some(s) => s.clone(),
            None => SmallVec::from_elem(
                Span { lo: self.stock_bottom_z, hi: self.top[idx] }, 1),
        }
    }

    /// Write a column back, keeping the dense/sidecar split canonical.
    fn store_column(&mut self, idx: usize, ix: u32, iy: u32, mut spans: SmallVec<[Span; 2]>) {
        merge_adjacent(&mut spans);
        match spans.as_slice() {
            // Cut clean through — no solid left. Dense top drops to the
            // floor; the mesh already collapses the underside at floorZ.
            [] => { self.top[idx] = self.stock_bottom_z; self.undercut.remove(&(idx as u32)); }
            // Still one full-height span ⇒ pure top-down. Dense-only.
            [s] if s.lo <= self.stock_bottom_z => {
                self.top[idx] = s.hi;
                self.undercut.remove(&(idx as u32));
            }
            // Interior void ⇒ sidecar; dense top mirrors the highest surface.
            _ => {
                self.top[idx] = spans.last().unwrap().hi;
                self.undercut.insert(idx as u32, spans);
            }
        }
        self.mark_dirty(ix, iy);   // unchanged from Heightmap
    }
}
```

## 3. Carve one cell — fast path preserved, undercut path added

```rust
impl DexelField {
    #[inline]
    fn carve_cell(&mut self, ix: u32, iy: u32, removed_lo: f32, removed_hi: f32) {
        let idx = (iy as usize) * (self.cols as usize) + (ix as usize);

        // FAST PATH — removal reaches the top and the column is still a
        // single full-height span ⇒ this is a plain top-down cut. This is
        // LITERALLY today's `lower_at_unchecked`: monotone-min on the dense
        // array, zero sidecar cost. 3-axis jobs never leave this branch.
        if removed_hi >= self.top[idx] && !self.undercut.contains_key(&(idx as u32)) {
            if removed_lo < self.top[idx] {
                self.top[idx] = removed_lo;
                self.mark_dirty(ix, iy);
            }
            return;
        }

        // SLOW PATH — interior removal (undercut) or already multi-span.
        let mut spans = self.spans_for(idx);
        subtract_interval(&mut spans, removed_lo, removed_hi);
        self.store_column(idx, ix, iy, spans);
    }
}

/// 1-D interval difference: remove [a,b] from a sorted disjoint span list,
/// splitting a span when the removal is strictly interior.
fn subtract_interval(spans: &mut SmallVec<[Span; 2]>, a: f32, b: f32) {
    let mut out: SmallVec<[Span; 2]> = SmallVec::new();
    for s in spans.iter().copied() {
        if b <= s.lo || a >= s.hi { out.push(s); continue; }   // disjoint
        if a > s.lo { out.push(Span { lo: s.lo, hi: a }); }    // keep below
        if b < s.hi { out.push(Span { lo: b, hi: s.hi }); }    // keep above
        // else the removal covers this span entirely — drop it
    }
    *spans = out;
}
```

## 4. Sweep integration — mirror of `sweep_chord_carve`

The existing loop passes `(ix, iy, r, cutter_pz, dz)`. The dexel variant ignores
`dz` and asks the tool for its removed interval at `r`:

```rust
pub fn sweep_chord_carve_dexel(
    field: &mut DexelField, segment: &ToolpathSegment, profile: &ToolProfile,
) -> u32 {
    // identical preamble to sweep_chord_carve: dragoff shift, air-skip
    // (from.z >= top_z && to.z >= top_z), engagement-depth clamp.
    let floor = profile.max_engagement_depth().map(|d| field.top_z - d);
    let layout = HeightmapLayout::of_field(field);
    let mut touched = 0u32;
    for_each_swept_cell(&layout, segment, profile, |ix, iy, r, cutter_pz, _dz| {
        if let Some((lo, hi)) = profile.eval_interval(r) {
            let pz = cutter_pz as f32;
            let removed_lo = floor.map_or(pz + lo, |f| (pz + lo).max(f));
            let removed_hi = if hi.is_infinite() { field.top_z.max(pz) } else { pz + hi };
            field.carve_cell(ix, iy, removed_lo, removed_hi);
            touched += 1;
        }
    });
    touched
}
```

**Partial-t bitwise identity is preserved.** `subtract_interval` + `merge_adjacent`
make removal associative and idempotent, so `[0,t]` then `[t,1]` yields the same
span list as `[0,1]` — the same guarantee `partial_advance_non_flat_no_drift`
(`sweep.rs:1305`) enforces today, now on intervals instead of `min()`.

## 5. What ships in Phase 1 vs stays deferred

- **Ships:** honest T-slot / dovetail undercuts (finite `form_upper`); dense
  fast path unchanged ⇒ zero regression for 3-axis; `merge_adjacent` +
  `subtract_interval` unit-tested against the old `min()` for single-span cols.
- **Phase 2 (flip):** carve a *second* span from below — `eval_interval` already
  yields the interval; just drive `cutter_pz` from `top_z - thickness` upward.
  One field ⇒ one watertight solid; unblocks `ivac-rt1.11`.
- **WASM/GL (incremental):** keep exposing `top` via the existing `data_ptr()`
  (unchanged zero-copy). Add sidecar buffers `undercut_keys: Vec<u32>` +
  CSR `undercut_offsets/spans` uploaded only when non-empty; frontend renders
  the top surface as today plus undercut walls from the sidecar. Dirty-AABB
  upload is untouched for the common case.
- **Checkpoint/replay:** snapshot = `top.clone()` + `undercut.clone()`; carving
  stays monotone (material only removed) so the 12-snapshot scheme is valid.

## 6. First landing PR (smallest reviewable slice)

1. Add `Span`, `subtract_interval`, `merge_adjacent` + unit tests (pure, no field).
2. Add `ToolProfile::eval_interval` (returns `(eval(r), +inf)` for all non-form
   kinds) + a test that it equals today's `eval` semantics on the top span.
3. Introduce `DexelField` as a thin wrapper whose `undercut` map is always empty
   in step 1 — prove byte-identical carves vs `Heightmap` on the existing sim
   test corpus (the fast path IS `lower_at_unchecked`).
4. Only then wire `form_upper` and let T-slot populate the sidecar.
```

description:
First slice of the text-engraving rework: introduce a first-class \`TextLayer\` entity in project state so text content / font / size / position / rotation become persistent project state instead of baked-at-add-time geometry.

## Scope

- New \`TextLayer\` interface in project.svelte.ts:
  \`\`\`ts
  interface TextLayer {
    id: number;
    kind: 'TEXT' | 'MTEXT';
    text: string;
    fontSource: { kind: 'bundled'; path: string }
              | { kind: 'user'; filename: string; bytes_b64: string };
    sizeMm: number;
    origin: { x: number; y: number };
    rotationDeg: number;
    letterSpacingMm: number;
    lineSpacingMm: number;
    alignment: 'left' | 'center' | 'right';
    singleLine: boolean;
  }
  \`\`\`
- \`textLayers: TextLayer[]\` field on ProjectState
- Mutators: \`addTextLayer\`, \`updateTextLayer\`, \`deleteTextLayer\` (command pattern matching addOperationCommand)
- Undo support via History wrappers
- Persistence in snapshot() / restore() for .wiac-project.json
- No UI yet — just the data model. AddTextDialog still bakes segments for now; phase 3 migrates it.

## Decisions baked in

- Legacy projects with already-baked text stay as static geometry. Migration is out of scope.
- User-loaded TTFs persist as base64 bytes inside the project file (portability over file-size).
- Live re-render cadence is a phase-2 concern.

## Acceptance criteria

- New project starts with \`textLayers: []\`.
- addTextLayer / updateTextLayer / deleteTextLayer all undo/redo cleanly.
- Save → reload → TextLayers round-trip identically.
- Existing 148 frontend tests still pass; add round-trip unit tests for save/restore.

## Out of scope

- Pipeline rendering (phase 2)
- Sidebar UI (phase 3)
- DXF TEXT/MTEXT preserved as TextLayer (phase 4)

## What

Multi-level undo / redo for every project state mutation. Currently wiac has no undo — accidentally deleting an operation, dragging a tab to a wrong contour, or picking the wrong tool means the user has to manually reverse the change (or reload the project file). For a tool people use to edit complex projects, this is a hard requirement; ten-step sequences with a misclick on step 9 are routine.

## Replace / Change / Extend

- **EXTEND** `frontend/src/lib/state/project.svelte.ts` with a history stack and undo/redo APIs.
- **EXTEND** every state-mutation site to wrap mutations in a single `history.transaction(label, fn)` call.
- **No replacements.** State shape unchanged; history is additive.

## Approach: command-pattern (recommended) vs. snapshot-pattern

Two approaches; pick command-pattern.

**Snapshot-pattern**: deep-clone the entire project state on every mutation. Simple to implement (~100 LOC) but expensive on memory and clones for big projects (tens of MB per snapshot × 50 history depth = GB-scale). Reject for v1; might be acceptable as fallback for ops we can't model as commands.

**Command-pattern (recommended)**: every mutation is a `Command { apply, revert, label }` value. Apply pushes onto the undo stack; undo pops to the redo stack. Memory cost is the *delta* per command, typically small (one operation's payload, one tool entry, etc.).

```ts
// frontend/src/lib/state/history.svelte.ts (new file)

export interface Command {
  label: string;       // "Add operation 'Profile of layer 0'"
  apply(state: ProjectState): void;
  revert(state: ProjectState): void;
}

export class History {
  private undoStack: Command[] = [];
  private redoStack: Command[] = [];
  private depth: number;

  constructor(depth = 100) { this.depth = depth; }

  exec(cmd: Command, state: ProjectState) {
    cmd.apply(state);
    this.undoStack.push(cmd);
    if (this.undoStack.length > this.depth) this.undoStack.shift();
    this.redoStack.length = 0;
  }

  undo(state: ProjectState) {
    const cmd = this.undoStack.pop();
    if (!cmd) return false;
    cmd.revert(state);
    this.redoStack.push(cmd);
    return true;
  }

  redo(state: ProjectState) { /* mirror */ }

  /** Group multiple commands as one undo step (e.g. "Add Text" creates
      both geometry + an op; one Ctrl+Z reverts both). */
  transaction(label: string, fn: (push: (cmd: Command) => void) => void): void;
}
```

### Integration

Every mutation in `project.svelte.ts` (and components that reach into the state directly) wraps the change in a `Command`. Helper factories generate the standard ones:

```ts
// frontend/src/lib/state/commands.ts (new file)

export function addOpCommand(op: Operation): Command {
  return {
    label: `Add operation '${op.name}'`,
    apply: state => { state.project.operations.push(structuredClone(op)); },
    revert: state => { state.project.operations.pop(); },
  };
}

export function deleteOpCommand(opId: u32): Command {
  let savedIdx: number | null = null;
  let savedOp: Operation | null = null;
  return {
    label: `Delete operation`,
    apply: state => {
      savedIdx = state.project.operations.findIndex(o => o.id === opId);
      if (savedIdx >= 0) {
        savedOp = structuredClone(state.project.operations[savedIdx]);
        state.project.operations.splice(savedIdx, 1);
      }
    },
    revert: state => {
      if (savedIdx !== null && savedOp) {
        state.project.operations.splice(savedIdx, 0, savedOp);
      }
    },
  };
}

// + setOpField, addTool, deleteTool, moveTab, setStockSize, etc.
```

### Coalescing

Numeric inputs (drag a slider, type into a number field) generate hundreds of mini-commands per second. Coalesce: if the next command is the *same kind targeting the same id within ~500ms*, merge into a single undo entry. Stop coalescing on focus-out or any other-kind command.

### Persistence

Don't persist the history to disk — undo crosses session is rarely useful and complicates the project format. Document as "undo history is per-session". Ctrl+Z after reopening a project file does nothing.

## UI

### Keyboard shortcuts

`App.svelte`:
- `Ctrl+Z` / `Cmd+Z` → `history.undo(state)`. If `undoStack.length === 0`: subtle shake animation, no-op.
- `Ctrl+Shift+Z` / `Cmd+Shift+Z` → `history.redo(state)`.
- `Ctrl+Y` → also redo (Windows muscle memory).

### Edit menu

Add an Edit menu in the top toolbar (or extend if present):
- "Undo: Add operation" (label dynamic from top of stack); disabled when empty.
- "Redo: …"; disabled when redo stack empty.

### History panel (optional)

Behind a settings flag: a side panel showing the undo stack as a list of labels. Clicking an entry undoes/redoes back to that point. Useful for power users; defer the UI to follow-up if non-trivial.

## Tests

- `history.svelte.ts::tests`:
  - `exec` then `undo` reverts the state cleanly.
  - 50 mutations + 50 undos = clean initial state.
  - Coalescing: 100 rapid same-kind commands → 1 undo step.
  - `redo` after `undo` restores. New `exec` clears redo stack.
- Integration: clicking "Add Operation" then Ctrl+Z removes the op without leaving an orphan entry in the operations list.
- Integration: undo across compound transactions (Add Text creates geometry + op; one Ctrl+Z reverts both).

## Time budget

- History core + tests: 4-5 days.
- Wrapping ~30 mutation sites in commands: 5-7 days. Most of the work.
- Coalescing + transactions: 2-3 days.

## Dependencies

- None hard. Pairs naturally with **`wiaconstructor-rt1.5`** (Add Text dialog) since the Text dialog explicitly notes the need for compound undo. If undo lands first, Add Text gets it for free; if Add Text lands first, ship a TODO note and circle back.

## Open questions

- Reach: every Svelte component currently mutates state directly via `state.project.foo = bar`. Audit needed. Some lint rule (eslint custom plugin? grep'able pattern?) to prevent future direct mutations from bypassing history.
- Sim playback: undo across "I scrubbed the playback to t=0.5" is annoying — playback isn't a project mutation, it's view state. Decide explicitly: view-state changes don't go through history.
- Selection state: changing the 2D selection isn't a project mutation either; same exclusion. Document.

## Acceptance

- [ ] History core ships with tests + coalescing + transactions.
- [ ] All ~30 mutation sites wrapped in commands.
- [ ] Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y / Ctrl+Shift+Z work everywhere.
- [ ] Edit menu shows current-action labels.
- [ ] No-op undo when stack empty (subtle shake, no error).
- [ ] 50-step undo→redo cycle leaves project state byte-identical.
- [ ] Documented in `frontend/src/lib/state/history.svelte.ts` JSDoc.

## What

Detect when a G0 (rapid) move would pass through stock material — i.e. the cutter would crash into uncut material at rapid speed. The current `sweep_segment` (`crates/wiac-core/src/sim/sweep.rs:35`) explicitly skips Rapid moves with a comment "the cutter is in air"; that's true *if* the move stays above the heightmap top. When a rapid is below `top_z` AND any cell along its swept footprint is still ≥ the rapid's Z, the cutter slams into stock at full speed. That's a tool-snap-and-fixture-eat moment.

## Replace / Change / Extend

- **CHANGE** `sweep_segment` (`crates/wiac-core/src/sim/sweep.rs:40-46`) so Rapid segments don't *short-circuit* — instead, they run the same cell-walk but in *check-only* mode (no cell modification) and emit a `RapidThroughMaterial` warning if any cell is above the segment's commanded Z.
- **EXTEND** `Heightmap` with a `query_max_z(ix0, iy0, ix1, iy1) -> f32` so the rapid path can fast-check the AABB without iterating per cell when the result is "definitely fine" (max Z below rapid Z).
- **No replacements** of the existing material-removal logic.

## Algorithm

`crates/wiac-core/src/sim/rapid_check.rs` (new file) holds the check, called from `sweep_segment`:

```rust
pub enum RapidCheck {
    Clear,
    Collision { worst_x: f64, worst_y: f64, worst_z: f32, segment_pz: f64 },
}

pub fn check_rapid_against_stock(
    heightmap: &Heightmap,
    segment: &ToolpathSegment,
    profile: ToolProfile,
) -> RapidCheck {
    debug_assert!(matches!(segment.kind, MoveKind::Rapid));

    // Fast reject: if min(from.z, to.z) is above heightmap.top_z, the
    // cutter never approaches the stock. (top_z is the *uncut* top; the
    // current heightmap may have been lowered locally.)
    let pz_min = segment.from.z.min(segment.to.z);
    if pz_min as f32 >= heightmap.top_z {
        return RapidCheck::Clear;
    }

    // Walk the same (ix, iy) cells `sweep_segment` would, project each
    // cell-center to (r, t), reconstruct cutter_pz at t. Compare against
    // the heightmap's stored Z for that cell.
    //
    // … same projection loop as sweep_segment, but instead of lower_at,
    // do `if heightmap.sample_cell(ix, iy) > cutter_pz_for_cell { ... }`.
    //
    // Track the worst (highest cell, lowest cutter_pz) and return.
}
```

Two correctness points:

- A rapid that *climbs* (from a deep pos to a high one) is fine if the climb completes before any obstruction. The check evaluates at each cell's `t` parameter, so a rising Z naturally clears the moment it's high enough — no special case.
- The cell test is `cell_z > cutter_pz + tool_profile.eval(r)` (use the same `eval(r)` that `sweep_segment` uses for material removal). On a V-bit, the cutter only "occupies" a thin spear at low r; on an endmill, the full disc.

## Wire-up

In `sweep_segment` (line 40-42 currently), replace the early-return-on-Rapid with:

```rust
if matches!(segment.kind, MoveKind::Rapid) {
    if let RapidCheck::Collision { .. } = check_rapid_against_stock(heightmap, segment, profile) {
        warnings.push(Warning::RapidThroughMaterial { /* segment_idx, position, … */ });
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This requires plumbing a `&mut Vec<Warning>` (or a sender) into `sweep_segment`. That's a signature change; do it as a careful refactor (most existing call sites are `sweep_range` and frontend driver — both go through the same chain). The exact Warning channel design lands in Child 6 (sim warnings stream); ship a stub here that just collects into a `Vec<Warning>`.

## UI

Until Child 6 lands, **just log** to `tracing::warn!` and add a visible chip on the affected operation. Wire-up to a real per-segment overlay is part of Child 6.

For visual debug: when a rapid collision is detected, mark the offending segment in the 3D preview with a red color band. `Scene3D.svelte`'s segment-color pipeline already exists for the toolpath; extend with a red override for `kind == Rapid && has_collision`.

## Tests

- `rapid_check.rs::tests`:
  - Rapid from `(0,0,5)` to `(10,0,5)` with `top_z = 0`: returns Clear.
  - Rapid from `(0,0,-2)` to `(10,0,-2)` with `top_z = 0` and uncut heightmap: returns Collision (every cell along the path is above -2).
  - Rapid from `(0,0,5)` to `(10,0,-2)` (descending late): returns Clear if the heightmap is uncut up to the descent point. Verify by lowering only the cells past `t=0.7` to -3 in setup.
  - Pure plunge (zero XY length): degenerates to "is the cell at (from.x, from.y) higher than `to.z`?" — confirm.
- Pipeline integration: add a project that programs a rapid through uncut stock, run sim, confirm the warning fires. Add a regression test using one of the existing `*.vc-project.json` fixtures (force-edit a known-good rapid into a known-bad one).

## Time budget

- Algorithm + tests: 3-4 days. Most of the heavy lifting is reusing `sweep_segment`'s projection loop unchanged.
- Wire-up + UI red-coloring: 2-3 days.

## Dependencies

- Soft dependency on Child 6 (warnings stream) for the final UX. Ship the warning *collection* here; surfacing is wired up by Child 6.
- No hard dependencies.

## Open questions

- Tolerance: "rapid Z exactly equals stock Z" — collision or clear? Pick *clear* (use strict `>`), matches typical machinist intent ("rapid to surface, then plunge"). Document the choice.
- Stock-edge case: rapid passes outside the heightmap footprint. Currently `world_aabb_to_cells` clamps; outside cells aren't tested. That's correct (cutter is in air). Confirm in test.

## Acceptance

- [ ] `RapidCheck` ships with the unit tests above.
- [ ] `sweep_segment` collects rapid-collision warnings without changing material-removal behavior on cut moves.
- [ ] Visible red overlay in 3D preview for collided rapids.
- [ ] Existing sim regression tests still pass.
- [ ] Documented in `crates/wiac-core/src/sim/rapid_check.rs` module docs.

Foundation for 602: a Heightmap struct in wiac-core that owns the cell grid + a TipProfile per tool kind.

Architecture: implemented in Rust under crates/wiac-core/src/sim/heightmap.rs (chosen for performance — JS Float32Array loops are decent but Rust wins by 5-20× on tight per-cell numeric work). Exposed to the frontend via wiac-wasm in a follow-up sub-issue.

API:

  pub struct Heightmap {
    pub origin: Point2,
    pub cell: f64,
    pub cols: u32,
    pub rows: u32,
    pub top_z: f32,
    pub data: Vec<f32>,             // row-major
  }

  impl Heightmap {
    pub fn new(stock: &Stock, cell: f64) -> Self;
    pub fn lower_at(&mut self, ix: u32, iy: u32, z: f32);
    pub fn sample(&self, x: f64, y: f64) -> f32;
    pub fn reset(&mut self);
    pub fn dirty_aabb(&self) -> Option<(IRange, IRange)>;
    pub fn clear_dirty(&mut self);
    pub fn data_ptr(&self) -> *const f32;       // for wasm-bindgen zero-copy
