== Pre-flight facts (already checked for you) ==
- The 3 arms differ ONLY in the Post they build: Linuxcnc => linuxcnc::Post::new(); Grbl => grbl::Post::with_dynamic_laser(project.machine.laser_dynamic_power); Hpgl => hpgl::Post::new(). All 11 other args are identical.
- PostProcessor (crate::gcode, trait in crates/ivac-core/src/gcode.rs) is OBJECT-SAFE: it has ZERO generic methods (verified). So &mut dyn PostProcessor is technically possible.
- BUT: run_per_op calls many other <P: PostProcessor> fns (run_op_driver, run_standard_op, store_op_cache, apply_cached_op, emit_program_only_op, SpecialtyKind::run, and the per-kind drivers). Switching run_per_op to take &mut dyn forces a ?Sized bound to CASCADE through all of them, AND trades static dispatch for dynamic dispatch in the hot emit loop. That is a large, higher-risk change for a P3 cleanup whose perf benefit is dubious.

== RECOMMENDED approach: a local macro (contained, zero behavior change, keeps monomorphization) ==
Replace the 3 triplicated calls with one macro so a new run_per_op arg is a ONE-LINE edit. In run_pipeline, just above 'let gcode = match post_kind':

    // Single source for the run_per_op call; each arm only varies the
    // concrete Post. Add a run_per_op argument here once, not 3x.
    macro_rules! run_with_post {
        ($post:expr) => {
            run_per_op(
                &project, &objects, &header_setup, $post, &stats_collector,
                progress, n_ops, &mut warnings, sink, cancel, cache, post_tag,
            )
        };
    }
    let gcode = match post_kind {
        PostProcessorKind::Linuxcnc => run_with_post!(&mut linuxcnc::Post::new()),
        PostProcessorKind::Grbl => run_with_post!(&mut grbl::Post::with_dynamic_laser(
            project.machine.laser_dynamic_power,
        )),
        PostProcessorKind::Hpgl => run_with_post!(&mut hpgl::Post::new()),
    }?;

That removes the textual triplication (the issue) without touching run_per_op's signature or any downstream generic fn. Do NOT pursue the &mut dyn rewrite unless explicitly asked — see the cascade caveat above.

