Where: crates/wiac-core/src/cam/vcarve_emit.rs:138-141.

Problem: The outer ratchet loop break condition is:
    current_level -= dpp;
    if current_level < target_z.iter().fold(0, |a, &b| a.min(b)) - dpp {
        break;
    }
i.e. exit when current_level drops a full DPP below the deepest target. Combined with the `progressed` flag (line 127), the loop typically exits when no forward sweep made progress.

But: with floating-point dpp accumulation, current_level can land EXACTLY on the deepest target_z minus some accumulated rounding error and the comparison fails inconsistently. More importantly, when target_z is non-uniform across the polyline (typical V-Carve), the loop relies on `progressed=true` to keep going; if a single forward sweep happens to find no cut on some pass (e.g. all points already at their target), the loop exits even if there are deeper points still uncut.

Hard to trigger in practice but plausible with discretization quirks. The redundant break condition at line 139-141 also depends on dpp without using level count.

Real-world impact: Edge-case incomplete cuts where some points in the medial axis end up at a shallower-than-target depth, leaving uneven groove floors. Hard to spot without comparing gcode against geometric target.

