```rust
pub fn build_qr(
    data: &str,
    module_size_mm: f64,           // physical size of one QR module
    error_correction: QrLevel,     // L / M / Q / H
    include_quiet_zone: bool,
    origin: Point2,
    layer: &str,
) -> Result<Vec<Segment>, Error> {
    let code = QrCode::with_error_correction_level(data, ec_level(error_correction))?;
    let modules = code.to_colors();
    let n = code.width();   // n × n grid

    // Quiet zone (4 modules of background per spec) — reserve, don't draw.
    let offset = if include_quiet_zone { 4 } else { 0 };

    let mut segs = Vec::new();
    for y in 0..n {
        for x in 0..n {
            if modules[y * n + x] == Color::Dark {
                // Emit a closed square as 4 line segments.
                let x0 = origin.x + (x as f64 + offset as f64) * module_size_mm;
                let y0 = origin.y + (y as f64 + offset as f64) * module_size_mm;
                push_square(&mut segs, x0, y0, module_size_mm, layer);
            }
        }
    }
    Ok(segs)
}
```

Standard QR error correction levels: L=7%, M=15%, Q=25%, H=30% recoverable. Default M.

## Optimization: merge adjacent ON modules

A 33×33 V-2 QR (typical URL) has up to ~500 ON modules → 2000 segments → bloat. Run-length-encode horizontally adjacent ON modules into single rectangles per row before emitting; reduces typical segment count by 5-10×. Quick to implement; significant downstream benefit for the chaining/offset pipeline.

```rust
// after building the modules grid:
for y in 0..n {
    let mut x = 0;
    while x < n {
        if modules[y * n + x] == Dark {
            let start = x;
            while x < n && modules[y * n + x] == Dark { x += 1; }
            // Emit one rectangle from (start, y) to (x, y+1).
            push_rectangle(&mut segs, start, x, y, module_size_mm, layer);
        } else {
            x += 1;
        }
    }
}
```

This produces "horizontal stripes" — visually no different to the QR scanner, geometrically fewer segments.

